This is the human-readable path from zero to a signed proof. For a paste-ready
agent prompt, use [/install](/install) or run `thwip setup` after the CLI is on
your PATH.

{% callout type="note" title="Requirements" %}
Node 22 or newer. A GitHub account. Repository admin access when you enable a
project on the org.
{% /callout %}

{% steps %}
{% step title="Install the CLI" %}

The package is `@recallnet/thwip`. The command is `thwip`.

```bash
npm install -g @recallnet/thwip
thwip --help
```

Prefer no global install? Use `npx @recallnet/thwip` wherever this guide says
`thwip`.

{% /step %}
{% step title="Sign in" %}

Device flow only. Run this in **your** terminal so the browser approval is
yours—do not hand a blocked login to an unattended agent.

```bash
thwip login
```

Approve the code at the printed URL. Wait until the CLI reports signed in.

{% /step %}
{% step title="Register a signing key" %}

Proofs are signed with an OpenSSH ed25519 key enrolled as a **Signing Key** on
the same GitHub account the CLI uses.

```bash
thwip keys status
```

If no key exists:

```bash
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
```

Print the public half and add it at
[github.com/settings/ssh/new](https://github.com/settings/ssh/new):

```bash
cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
```

{% callout type="warning" title="Common mistake" %}
GitHub defaults new keys to **Authentication**. thwip only reads **Signing**
keys. Wrong type means proofs fail with a revoked-or-missing key style error
even though the key “exists” on your account.
{% /callout %}

Confirm:

```bash
thwip keys status
```

You want `registeredWithGithub: true` for the key that will sign.

{% /step %}
{% step title="Open the dashboard and enable a project" %}

1. Go to [thwip.recall.net/dashboard](https://thwip.recall.net/dashboard).
2. Install the thwip GitHub App on your org if you have not already.
3. Enable a repository. Organization defaults apply until a protected
   `thwip.yaml` on the default branch overrides them.

Repository bootstrap is a **separate** agent prompt on the project page. Machine
setup ends here; repo setup starts there.

{% /step %}
{% step title="Initialize and run in a clone" %}

From a checkout of a project thwip already knows:

```bash
thwip init --required "lint,typecheck,test"
thwip run
```

`thwip run` executes the required checks, signs the proof, and amends the commit
trailer. Push the branch and open a PR as you usually would. The App evaluates
the exact head subject.

{% /step %}
{% /steps %}

## What success looks like

- `thwip keys status` shows a registered signing key for your GitHub login.
- The org appears under the dashboard after App install.
- A PR on an enabled project gets a thwip proof check that can pass without
  replaying your suite in Actions (fast profile), or with GitHub-run checks
  (remote profile).

